WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:11.406
A board passes every software test but crashes randomly in the field. The culprit is one bad memory cell. March tests are how factories catch it.

00:00:12.606 --> 00:00:13.535
Pause and think.

00:00:13.935 --> 00:00:17.070
Why does a march test go both up and down through memory?

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:24.784
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:25.984 --> 00:00:31.777
This video explains how chip and board engineers find broken memory cells, using a march test.

00:00:32.177 --> 00:00:37.669
We start with an analogy, define the fault types, then step through March C minus.

00:00:38.869 --> 00:00:42.747
Picture a hallway of lockers, and you must prove every one works.

00:00:43.147 --> 00:00:47.106
The simple plan: put a note in every locker, then read every note back.

00:00:47.506 --> 00:00:52.626
A broken lock that is stuck always shows the same note. The simple plan catches that.

00:00:53.026 --> 00:00:57.565
But some lockers disturb their neighbour when you open them. That is a coupling fault.

00:00:57.965 --> 00:01:05.512
To catch a neighbour on either side, you must walk the hallway in both directions, writing and reading as you go. That is a march test.

00:01:06.712 --> 00:01:08.024
Four words to know.

00:01:08.424 --> 00:01:11.976
A stuck-at fault: the cell always reads the same value.

00:01:12.376 --> 00:01:15.790
A transition fault: the cell can't change in one direction.

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:21.286
A coupling fault: writing one cell, the aggressor, flips another cell, the victim.

00:01:21.686 --> 00:01:28.327
A march element: one pass over every address, going up or down, with the same reads and writes at each cell.

00:01:29.527 --> 00:01:32.209
Here is March C minus, element by element.

00:01:32.609 --> 00:01:35.570
First, write zero everywhere, in any order.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:40.556
Going up, read zero then write one. A stuck-at-one cell fails its read here.

00:01:40.956 --> 00:01:46.354
Still going up, read one then write zero. A cell that can't fall from one to zero fails here.

00:01:46.754 --> 00:01:52.803
Now going down, read zero, write one. Writes from higher addresses reach victims below before they are read.

00:01:53.203 --> 00:01:55.734
Going down again, read one, write zero.

00:01:56.134 --> 00:02:05.236
Finally read zero everywhere. Ten operations per cell in total, and in the lesson's model a write-all, read-all test misses the coupling fault this catches.

00:02:06.436 --> 00:02:07.400
Let's recap.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.144
A simple write-all, read-all test misses some coupling faults.

00:02:11.544 --> 00:02:15.085
The direction of each pass decides which neighbour you can catch.

00:02:15.485 --> 00:02:18.515
March C minus costs ten operations per cell.

00:02:18.915 --> 00:02:21.504
Try it yourself in lesson thirty seven point one.

00:02:22.684 --> 00:02:23.938
Back to the question.

00:02:24.338 --> 00:02:27.473
Why does a march test go both up and down through memory?

00:02:27.873 --> 00:02:30.880
Some coupling faults only appear in one address order.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:32.950
Quick recap.

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:35.405
Write and read patterns in order.

00:02:35.805 --> 00:02:37.814
Cover stuck-at and coupling faults.

00:02:38.214 --> 00:02:40.234
March C minus is the classic.
